# modules/network/checks-banner.rules - the NET-07 script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; data/scoursh-network-scan-design/report.md
# §3.2 item 1, §5.1, the NET-07 row in its §7 staged plan). Owned by
# modules/network/banner.sh.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's
# `checks-<name>.rules` row (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) - matching
# modules/network/checks-reachability.rules (NET-06), never a shared
# `checks.rules` two peers could collide on.
#
# ONE CHECK ID, unlike NET-06's two: report.md §5.1's own table gives this
# probe exactly one row, `NET-SVC-BANNER_DISCLOSURE-01`, because unlike
# NET-PORT-*'s two genuinely different verdicts (a real exposure vs an
# inventory-hygiene observation), a banner disclosing a product name alone
# and a banner disclosing a product AND version are the SAME finding at two
# strengths of the same fact, not two different facts - net_banner_identify_
# text (modules/network/banner_engine.sh) reports both under this one id,
# with the version named in the evidence when one was disclosed.
#
# The type tag is `passive` (report.md §5.1's own table for this check, and
# modules/network/engine.sh's own phase-table floor, `banner.sh:passive`):
# the probe reads only what a listener volunteers unprompted on connect and
# sends zero bytes - the opposite of NET-06's own `safe-active` tag, whose
# header explains why a bare connect attempt earns that tag instead (a real
# connection to a port the operator did not necessarily expect a scanner to
# knock on). Sending nothing at all on that same connection is what earns
# this check the weaker tag.
#
# `coverage-scope: target` is NET's cell (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's own
# NET row: the config/scope.conf target id), identical to every other NET-*
# check.

id: NET-SVC-BANNER_DISCLOSURE-01
title: Network service discloses product/version unprompted
script: banner.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: A service greeting that names its own product and exact
  version lets an attacker select exploits for that product and version
  without probing for them first. Where the software supports it, suppress
  or generalise the banner (an SSH `Banner`/version-string override, an
  SMTP `smtpd_banner` set to a generic string, an FTP daemon login-message
  override) so it no longer names the product or the version. This is
  defence in depth, not a fix on its own: keep the component patched on its
  own schedule regardless of whether the banner is suppressed.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
